<h2 id="start-this-month">How to start a side hustle in Los Angeles this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Choose your zone.</strong> Pick the part of LA you can reach in 20 minutes and build there. Driving across town for a $40 job is a losing trade.</li>
  <li><strong>Pick one hustle that matches your hours.</strong> Not sure which fits? Take the <a href="/tools/side-hustle-quiz/">side hustle quiz</a> and commit to one idea for 90 days.</li>
  <li><strong>Register with the city.</strong> Apply for your Business Tax Registration Certificate with the LA Office of Finance and calendar the annual renewal deadline so you keep the small business exemption.</li>
  <li><strong>Get visible locally.</strong> Set up a Google Business Profile, post on Nextdoor and in neighborhood Facebook groups, and ask your first three clients for reviews.</li>
  <li><strong>Price for LA costs.</strong> Factor in gas, parking and insurance. Rates that work in Fresno will not work in Santa Monica.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate your money.</strong> Open a separate checking account, track mileage and set aside a tax percentage from every payment.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="california-taxes">California taxes, AB5 and LA business rules</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> California's brackets run from 1% to 12.3%, plus an additional 1% on taxable income above $1 million. Self-employment profit is taxed as ordinary income, and you pay quarterly estimates to the Franchise Tax Board.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, on top of federal income tax. Estimate your set-aside with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>AB5:</strong> California uses the strict "ABC test" to decide whether a worker is an employee. If you freelance for businesses, keep your own clients, tools and schedule. App-based drivers fall under Proposition 22 instead, which the California Supreme Court upheld in 2024.</li>
  <li><strong>LA Business Tax Registration Certificate:</strong> You are considered to be doing business in the City of Los Angeles if you work there seven or more days a year, which covers most freelancers and gig workers who live in the city. Businesses with $100,000 or less in worldwide gross receipts can generally claim the small business exemption, but only with a timely annual renewal. Creative artists have a separate exemption threshold. Cities like Santa Monica, Pasadena and Burbank run their own licensing.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> If you sell physical goods, register for a seller's permit with the California Department of Tax and Fee Administration.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> The first year of business in Los Angeles is generally not covered by the small business exemption the way later years are, and late renewals lose it. Read the Office of Finance guidance or talk to a tax pro before you skip filing.</div>

